There are actually two separate issues that crept in.

1. The Schema Definition has degraded

Originally, every field was documented like this:

Field Name	Data Type	Key	Specification	Reason Field Exists
id	UUID	PK	NOT NULL	Unique identifier
tenant_id	UUID	FK → tenants.id	NOT NULL	Parent tenant
...	...	...	...	...

Notice every field had

Field Name
Data Type
Key Type
Constraints/Specification
Business reason

Later tables became

| Field | Type | Notes |

which is a major loss of information.

The following disappeared:

PK
FK
UNIQUE
Composite Keys
Nullable indication
NOT NULL
CHECK constraints
Default values
Business reason column

That absolutely should not happen.

For a database design document, the schema section is the single most important section.

2. The tables became shorter

This also happened.

Earlier tables (1–15 especially) contained much deeper discussion.

For example:

Section 10 used to explain

why field exists
business purpose
future extensibility
implementation rationale

Now it's often just

"Stores provider-specific configuration."

which is much weaker.

