Why this happened

It wasn't intentional.

As the conversation became very long, I started compressing sections to stay within the response length limits. The Markdown versions also became summaries rather than near-identical copies of the chat documentation.

That optimization is appropriate for some conversations, but it is the wrong tradeoff for your database design documentation.

For InsureIQ, this is a serious issue

Your objective isn't simply to "finish all 27 tables."

You're creating what is effectively:

an enterprise database design specification,
a DDD reference,
an implementation guide,
a compliance document,
and a future developer handbook.

For that purpose, every table should have consistent depth and structure.

If later tables are weaker than earlier ones, the document becomes inconsistent and less useful.

What I recommend going forward

For the remaining tables, I recommend returning to the original standard used for Tables 1–15.

Specifically:

Restore the full schema definition with:
Field Name
Data Type
Key Type (PK/FK/UK/CK)
Specification (NOT NULL, UNIQUE, DEFAULT, CHECK, etc.)
Reason Field Exists
Expand each of the 23 sections back to their original depth.
Include richer business rationale, DDD context, operational considerations, and implementation guidance rather than abbreviated summaries.
Keep the Markdown output as close as possible to the chat version, instead of turning it into a condensed reference.

I also recommend we go back and revise Tables 16–22 so they match the quality and consistency of Tables 1–15. If these are left in their current shortened form, Module 1 will have a noticeable drop in documentation quality starting from Table 16, which is not ideal for a long-term architecture document.

